<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anti-Socialist Laws</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Socialist Law, Sozialistengesetz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anti-Socialist Laws were a German law passed in October 1878 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>'s urging after two attempts on the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a>. It banned socialist and social democratic organisations, meetings and publications, although Social Democrats could still stand for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>. Renewed several times, it lapsed in 1890, by which time the Social Democratic Party had grown stronger.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws.jpg" alt="Anti-Socialist Laws" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichsgesetzblatt34_1878.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>These were pragmatic measures designed to undercut socialist appeal alongside the <b>Anti-Socialist Laws</b> of 1878.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1880s and 1890s the conservative German chancellor Otto von Bismarck put in place welfare measures first proposed by the Social Democrats, after trying to curb them with his <b>Anti-Socialist Laws</b>, and laid the ground for the first modern welfare state.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-democracy">Social democracy · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
